From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carlos Chulo <cchulo.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 06:32:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] HID: asus-ally: map the ROG Xbox Ally X20 Quick Access key
 (0xa0)

On the ROG Xbox Ally X20 (board RC74XA) the Quick Access button sends
vendor code 0xa0 on the N-KEY interface: "5a a0" on press, "5a 00" on
release. The Ally and Ally X send 0xa6 for the same function. Neither
ally_x_raw_event() nor the Ally config input path in ally_raw_event()
knows 0xa0, so the button does nothing on SteamOS.

Map 0xa0 alongside 0xa6:

- Default (qam_mode=0 / non-Steam) paths: report KEY_F16, the same key
  0xa6 produces and the one hid-asus documents as "ROG Ally QAM button".
  This applies both to the Ally X gamepad input device and to the "ASUS
  ROG Ally Config" input device.
- Ally X Steam mode (qam_mode=1, the driver's default): emit the Steam
  Quick Access chord (BTN_MODE then BTN_A), which the driver already
  uses for 0x38/0x93. Here 0xa0 deliberately does not copy 0xa6. In
  Steam mode 0xa6 becomes a bare BTN_MODE, which opens the Steam menu,
  and on the X20 the dedicated Xbox button already sends BTN_MODE
  through the gamepad interface. Copying 0xa6 would leave the X20 with
  two Steam-menu buttons and no Quick Access button.

hid-asus is not changed. Its asus_input_mapping() table also maps
vendor usage 0xa6 to KEY_F16, but with CONFIG_HID_ASUS_ALLY reachable
hid-asus skips the Ally endpoints 0x83, 0x87 and 0x04
(asus_probe() in hid-asus.c returns -ENODEV for them), and the 0x5a vendor
reports are delivered to hid-asus-ally. The other hid-asus devices
(ROG laptop keyboards) may use 0xa0 for something else, so a
hid-asus-wide mapping would be wrong too.

No device-table change is needed. The X20's N-KEY device enumerates as
0b05:1b4c (hidraw node 0003:0B05:1B4C.*), the same ID as the ROG Ally X
(USB_DEVICE_ID_ASUSTEK_ROG_NKEY_ALLY_X), so it already binds to this
driver.

Derived from:
- ovenham/rog-ally-x20-steamos-speaker-qam-fix (README button table
  and tools/x20-buttons.sh: 0xa0 on press, 0x00 on release; N-KEY
  device 0b05:1b4c),
  https://github.com/ovenham/rog-ally-x20-steamos-speaker-qam-fix
- drivers/hid/hid-asus.c in the same tree (0xa6 -> KEY_F16 "ROG Ally
  QAM button"; Ally endpoints skipped when CONFIG_HID_ASUS_ALLY is
  reachable)

Base: linux-integration 7.2.7-valve2 plus the previous patches in this
series. Applies without them as well.

TODO(hw): on an RC74XA confirm with hidraw that Quick Access sends 5a a0
and no other code, that lsusb shows 0b05:1b4c, and that with qam_mode=1
the button opens Steam's Quick Access menu exactly once per press.
---
 drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c b/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
index 8c1cd00..9947298 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-asus-ally.c
@@ -36,6 +36,14 @@
 #define ROG_ALLY_X_MIN_MCU 313
 #define ROG_ALLY_MIN_MCU 319
 
+/*
+ * Vendor key codes in byte 1 of 0x5a input reports on the N-KEY interface.
+ * The ROG Xbox Ally X20 (RC74XA) sends 0xa0 for its Quick Access button,
+ * where earlier Allys send 0xa6.
+ */
+#define ALLY_KEY_QAM 0xa6
+#define ALLY_X20_KEY_QAM 0xa0
+
 #define FEATURE_KBD_LED_REPORT_ID1 0x5d
 #define FEATURE_KBD_LED_REPORT_ID2 0x5e
 
@@ -1530,16 +1538,23 @@ static int ally_x_raw_event(struct ally_x_device *ally_x, struct hid_report *rep
 	else if (data[0] == 0x5A) {
 		if (ally_x->qam_btns_steam_mode) {
 			spin_lock_irqsave(&ally_x->lock, flags);
-			if ((data[1] == 0x38 || data[1] == 0x93) && !ally_x->update_qam_btn) {
+			/*
+			 * The X20 Quick Access key gets the Steam Quick Access
+			 * chord (Xbox+A): its 0xa6 equivalent below emits BTN_MODE
+			 * alone, which the X20's own Xbox button already does.
+			 */
+			if ((data[1] == 0x38 || data[1] == 0x93 ||
+			     data[1] == ALLY_X20_KEY_QAM) && !ally_x->update_qam_btn) {
 				ally_x->update_qam_btn = true;
 				if (ally_x->output_worker_initialized)
 					schedule_work(&ally_x->output_worker);
 			}
 			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ally_x->lock, flags);
 			/* Left/XBox button. Long press does ctrl+alt+del which we can't catch */
-			input_report_key(ally_x->input, BTN_MODE, data[1] == 0xA6);
+			input_report_key(ally_x->input, BTN_MODE, data[1] == ALLY_KEY_QAM);
 		} else {
-			input_report_key(ally_x->input, KEY_F16, data[1] == 0xA6);
+			input_report_key(ally_x->input, KEY_F16,
+					 data[1] == ALLY_KEY_QAM || data[1] == ALLY_X20_KEY_QAM);
 			input_report_key(ally_x->input, KEY_PROG1, data[1] == 0x38 || data[1] == 0x93);
 		}
 		/* QAM long press */
@@ -2517,7 +2532,8 @@ static int ally_raw_event(struct hid_device *hdev, struct hid_report *report, u8
 
 	if (cfg && !ally_x) {
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_PROG1, data[1] == 0x38);
-		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F16, data[1] == 0xA6);
+		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F16,
+				 data[1] == ALLY_KEY_QAM || data[1] == ALLY_X20_KEY_QAM);
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F17, data[1] == 0xA7);
 		input_report_key(cfg->input, KEY_F18, data[1] == 0xA8);
 		input_sync(cfg->input);
